<br />
<p>Ludo (latin: game) • pastiche (fr: pasta || a work that mimics another in admiration)
<br /><br />
What went through my mind when working on these pieces and the games they come from. A ∞ symbol means the track repeats itself.
<br /><br />
Ludopastiche was an advent-style project: during December, I’d uncover a game title, listen to its soundtracks, do some light research on the composers & the music production process, and make a track based on that information. Each track had to be finished, from scoring to the final product, on the same day it started [if I had unlimited free time between December 1st and 24th, this would’ve been done in exactly 24 days as intended].
<br /><br />
<h1>Day 1, Hasty Hedgehog Zone ∞</h1>
Going into this, I didn’t realize that the game order was randomized and sincerely thought that this game was picked for the inaugural prompt because it was one of my favorite franchises growing up. Figured it was a way to dip my toes into pure electronic work under a beloved franchise. Creating my very first synth was pretty fun, and admittedly I knew it was with this track that my compositional palette was changing.
<br /><br />
Source: Sonic the Hedgehog (Green Hill Zone)<br />

<h1>Day 2, Pet Strength Exhibition ∞</h1>
This one was so fun! It’s one of my favorites in the whole set. Wobbly synth, the one that appears in the second half, brings me so much joy. 
<br /><br />
Source: Pokemon (battle theme, mostly gen 1-3).<br /> 

<h1>Day 3, Visions of the Elements</h1>
Think this was the first day I struggled. Since I knew this team has scores for their music, I followed the same approach with making a score then supplementing it with electronics afterwards. Translating the score to something electronic/orchestral hybrid was rougher than I’d imagine, and I wasn’t confident in it at the time. Learning is valuable, though.
<br /><br />
Source: Genshin Impact (character themes)<br />

<h1>Day 4, Coin Received, Credit Dispensed ∞</h1>
It was difficult to decide which era of arcade music I wanted to emulate, but I eventually settled for Frogger, Space Invaders, and Pac Man. The latter two are there because they’re the oldest and I enjoyed playing them whenever I could. Frogger is here because it’s arguably the first adaptive game soundtrack and I live for that sort of composition.
<br /><br />
Source: Arcade machines<br />

<h1>Day 5, Lost in the Blocks</h1>

When I finished Day 4, it took a lot less time than I thought so I jumped right into this ambient one. At first, I thought the buzziness from the strings would be too prominent and I’d have to start from scratch. As I kept working on it and adding the other layers, I realized it occupies the brain just enough to demand focus, but leaves enough space to let the listener sink in. My mind was snapped into this piece, it was to the point where I didn't realize I made a 4 minute track already. Similar to thinking that you’re only going to spend five minutes on Minecraft.
<br /><br />
Source: Minecraft<br />

<h1>Day 6, Legend of Robusta</h1>

This one was made in an undisclosed coffee shop in Montecito, California. It took an hour to find a cafe, preferably with outlets, that wouldn’t close before 8 PM, and an additional hour to figure out why my bluetooth earbuds weren’t connecting to my scoring software, but eventually I got my caffeine fix and whipped this one up in a nice town.
<br /><br />
Source: Legend of Zelda<br />

<h1>Day 7, Angsty Tales from Under ∞</h1>

Found an online OC [original character] generator for this one, because I just knew it would exist. The seed 12072023-normal pulled aggressive and paranoid in the personality pool, so I used those words to guide the track. I unintentionally leaned into breakcore by the end of this one, and it made me want to create an actual breakcore track some time in the future.
<br /><br />
Source: Undertale<br />

<a href="https://perchance.org/undertale-oc-generator" target="_blank">OC Generator from perchance.org/undertale-oc-generator</a><br />


<h1>Day 8, Futuristic Falcon Racers ∞</h1>
This was the first day where I questioned why the sound between NES games and what I was creating wasn’t quite the same - it was at that point I realized I wasn’t downsampling my sounds when making them. In a span of decades, we went from trying to reduce the amount of space music would occupy on a game disk to full on symphonies in mind-bogglingly large games. The need to add a filter to mimic reduced quality is a funny one.
<br /><br />
Source: F-Zero <br />
<br />

<h1>Day 9, Thy Kingdom Come, Thy Will Be Done, In Realms, As It Is In Our Hearts</h1>
 <br /><br />
My reference materials mentioned that this game bounces between purely electronic pieces and epic orchestral ones. When listening to the OSTs [original soundtracks] between games, I realized how apt that description was. I never played a game in this franchise (although I should), but it always looked extremely dramatic compared to the average Disney franchise in 2002. I assumed that a potential plot point in one of its numerous games is coming close to the main antagonist, just to be stopped because you fall down or something. The dramatic part is the build up, and the ending is the protagonist contemplating what went wrong.

Source: Kingdom Hearts<br />

<h1>Day 10, Ephemeral Illusion</h1>
 
Again, I really wish I played a game from this franchise aside from a few hours of XIV. Again, as someone who didn’t really dive deep into the lore, it always looked extremely dramatic and Sephiroth’s Smash Bros crossover trailer locked that vibe in for me. To my knowledge, this is one of the first franchises to conceptualize orchestral concerts of video game music, and it’s no wonder that it did, its soundtracks merit such a feat.
<br />
Secretly, I want to switch the tracks for 9 and 10 around, it feels like it would fit better that way.
<br /><br />
Source: Final Fantasy<br />

<h1>Day 11, 17:30-18:30 ∞</h1>
New Horizons was incredibly addictive, in part because of what was happening globally during its release. One thing I noticed after sinking many, many hours into it was how I didn’t get tired of the music yet each track was still distinguishable enough from each other. I couldn’t see myself replacing an hourly track, but they’re also very present in the game, so I made a half-hour to half-hour track.
<br /><br />
Source: Animal Crossing (New Horizons & City Folk)<br />

<h1>Day 12, The First Rule is REDACTED ∞</h1>
You just don’t talk about it…
<br /><br />
Source: Street Fighter III<br />

Joke’s over, the other reason why I don’t talk about Street Fighter is because I suck at it. Picking an era of Street Fighter was also tricky, and I wanted to shy away from the arcade since the arcade prompt was already finished. Capturing the high energy of this game without being too distracting was fun, too.
<br />

<h1>Day 13, I Love Bees</h1>
 
My only exposure to this franchise was me, a 4-year-old, watching my babysitter’s roommate’s 10-year-old son play this game. The needler was my favorite thing to watch at the time, probably because it was pink. What stood out the most when listening to the first two games’ soundtracks was their equal attention to both rock and orchestral elements, with additional filters to boot. If you listen closely, there’s a reference to one of the major Halo 2 tracks in the middle of this piece. 
<br /><br />
I Love Bees (also known as ilovebees or ILB for short) was an alternate reality game (ARG) that served as both a real-world experience and viral marketing campaign for the release of developer Bungie's 2004 video game Halo 2. 
<br /><br />
Source: Halo 1 & 2<br />

<h1>Day 14, Esperto di Tubi e Cocco ∞</h1>
It’s always exciting to find a professional justification for listening to Mario music. The structure of the song and its orchestration are pretty consistent in most of the soundtracks spanning the first three games, but each tracks’ iconicity proves a certain point.

Pipe and coconut expert
<br /><br />
Source: Mario (SMB 1-3 but mostly 3 and a pinch of Sunshine)<br />

<h1>Day 15, Many Punks, Mostly Virtual ∞</h1>
This one intimidated me, I’ve never touched vaporwave before and every track in 2077 is nothing short of impressive. Heck, one of my lifetime favorite artists, Blue Stahli, is in the game and thinking about emulating skilled artists like him in a genre that I’ve never touched was a doubt that I had to quickly, and rightfully, squashed.
<br /><br />
Source: Cyberpunk 2077<br />

<h1>Day 16, at the stake</h1>
 
My only dealings with this franchise were the first season of the Netflix adaptation of The Witcher. Toss a Coin to Your Witcher is fantastic in its way to blend folky/medieval orchestration with modern tact and this led me to underestimate how much more medieval the games’ OSTs are. I’m going to be honest, it’s very hurdy-gurdy-centric but only by accident, it accidentally saved my sanity with figuring out how I was going to go forward with this
<br /><br />
Source: Witcher<br />

<h1>Day 17, Midnight of War</h1>
 
When listening to the OST for the first two tracks, I found it interesting that speech was left in the audio for some tracks. Should I have verified that this is actually how they presented the OST? Probably. Was it a good excuse to practice mixing audio into this piece? Absolutely.
<br />
I didn’t know what speech I was going to use, just that it had to be CC3.0 or CC4.0. This made finding a suitable voice clip somewhat tricky, as most ones are copyrighted or simply did not fit the mood of the game or track. Eventually I found a narration of Paul Revere’s Ride and ran with that one.
<br /><br />
Source: God of War (1, 2, remastered 1 and the most recent one). 
Audio by <a href="https://www.mikevendetti.com/" target="_blank">Mike Vendetti (mikevendetti.com)</a> via <a href="https://freesound.org/people/alphahog/sounds/54915/" target="_blank">freesound.org , freesound.org/people/alphahog/sounds/54915</a>
<br /><br />

<h1>Day 18, Project Assassins</h1>
 
There’s a lot of choral use in the original soundtrack and, frankly, I actually never had a choral VST (virtual instrument) in my library before this song. I threw it in at the last minute because it didn’t feel like it was representative of Assassin’s Creed unless it was in there by some capacity.
<br /><br />
Source: Assassin’s Creed (1, 2, 3)
<br />
<h1>Day 19, bloki i linii, oni padayut ∞</h1>
I’m considerably familiar with Theme A (Korobeiniki) and the temptation to simply cover something like Bach’s Little Fugue in G minor was pretty strong. Nevertheless, I did not, and it was fun figuring out how much energy I could pour into a puzzle track. Theme A is pretty energetic, so why can’t this one be too?
<br /><br />
blocks and lines, they fall
<br /><br />
Source: Tetris (GameBoy Advance)<br />

<h1>Day 20, (hi, I’m) Skyr</h1>
Given its large and almost infinite overworld expanse, the OST benefits from being largely atmospheric. Drums and strings were notably the foundations of this game (and, of all things, Techno Viking) so I laid the foundation with the strings, and completely forgot about the drums until the end of the track. 
<br /><br />
Source: Skyrim<br />

<h1>Day 21, AM2DMCA ∞</h1>
I was so happy that I could stop using strings as the foundation of these tracks, particularly the run between 16-20 (minus 19), it was tiring. Two prominent points stand out for Metroid’s soundtracks: the first game’s insistence of the music being diegetic (a part of the game) rather than separate from the environment, and the use of chordal instability to cause discomfort, with some ease brought back by taking a simple motif and making some modifications to it as the track progresses. My response to those two points without copying the techniques outright was to use a pedal tone to build chords while using simple blocks of melody. For whatever reason, the day I made this was one of my least creative-feeling days so I decided to jump into the anti-strings sentiment by focusing on the piano, adding a minimal amount of accent instruments on top, and going over the top with sound effects.
<br /><br />
AM2R, or Another Metroid 2 Remake, was a fanmade and fan-loved remake of Metroid 2 that ultimately was DMCA’d (Digital Millennium Copyright Act), or essentially copyrighted, out of existence. The purpose of the DMCA was to make sure it didn’t compete with the official Nintendo release of the Metroid 2 remaster.
<br /><br />
Source: Metroid<br />

<h1>Day 22, Mon animal et jeux vidéo favoris !!</h1>

Peter McConnell, one of my biggest musical inspirations, wrote the soundtrack for this series starting from the second game (the first soundtrack is also great, it’s just distinct as is the gameplay between S1 and 2+). A large part of my alarming addiction to this series was the audio responsiveness to the player; McConnell helped pioneer the technology behind responsive audio, and it would have been a shame to not take a stab at converting this track to a vertical composition exercise. It was tough figuring out how I was going to demonstrate three versions of a track in a single song, but I solved that by making four versions.
<br /><br />
My favorite animal and video game!! written in French because Paris shows up many, many times in this series.
Source: Sly Cooper (2-4)<br /><br />

Toggles between sneaky/normal/fighty as shown [in the official publication] and decided with the rules that I had to show the transition between normal->fight, normal->sneaky, and sneaky-> fight at least once. 

<h1>Day 23, Easy Peasy Demon Squeezy</h1>

Dark Souls sparsely decorates its atmosphere with music. Consequently, the presence of music serves to accentuate the moment. Leitmotifs add an additional way to highlight the story when you reach these points. It was tricky figuring out if I wanted to make my own fight or safe haven. I erred on the side of a safe haven but it came out sounding a bit sadder than that. Just pretend that you’re discovering that a safe haven has been decimated and, (spoiler alert), this track’s motif will come back in a major key because you helped rebuild an even better safe haven.
<br />
This is the 23rd title I had to make and when I made it, I forgot to consider the mood of the track. Doesn’t match the mood but it’s too good to give up.
<br /><br />Source: Dark Souls (1 & 2)<br />

<h1>Day 24, Skiing Down Peak Consumerism ∞</h1>
With how open-ended this one was, I had to make an executive decision to either show off my orchestral prowess, demonstrate what I’ve learned with the electronic tracks, or try something completely different. I only had one track that wasn’t orchestral or chiptune adjacent so I decided to try Lo-Fi. People dub this genre as a low-effort genre but I’ll have you know that I suffered for seven hours and ate dinner at 1 am because of this song, possibly the longest session I’ve spent on any track in this album. Particularly the drums, those made me suffer the most. You know what that means? I’ll do it again and make another lo-fi track.
<br /><br />
Source: None. In a world where I had no obligations, this would have been finished on Christmas Eve for a debut on Christmas, but that isn’t realistic. It was instead finished on Wednesday, February 21st, 2024. Merry Christmas. Al*xa also sang after that but I cut her off for copyright reasons (also because I couldn’t manage to make it fit).
<br />
<h1>BONUSES</h1>    

